bodyText:
Alaska law sets these tenant duties whatever the lease says. The tenant must:
- keep the part of the premises the tenant uses as clean and safe as its condition permits;
- dispose of ashes, rubbish, garbage and other waste cleanly and safely;
- keep plumbing fixtures as clean as their condition permits;
- use the electrical, plumbing, heating, kitchen and other facilities and appliances reasonably;
- not deliberately or negligently damage or remove any part of the premises, or knowingly let anyone else;
- not unreasonably disturb neighbors, or let guests do so;
- keep smoke and carbon monoxide detectors working;
- not change locks without first getting your written agreement and immediately giving you keys, except in an emergency when you cannot be reached after reasonable effort, and then give you keys and written notice within five days;
- avoid conduct that triggers a municipal excessive-police-response fee;
- keep occupancy within legal and lease limits;
- and not knowingly engage in, or knowingly permit others to engage in, prostitution or illegal drug, gambling or local-option alcohol activity.

At move-out the tenant must leave the premises in substantially the same condition, except for normal wear and tear.

Alaska sets your maintenance duties by statute, and a lease cannot waive them. You must:
- make all repairs and do whatever is necessary to put and keep the premises fit and habitable;
- keep common areas clean and safe;
- keep the electrical, plumbing, sanitary, heating, ventilating, air-conditioning, kitchen and other facilities and appliances you supply, including elevators, in good and safe working order;
- provide waste receptacles and arrange removal;
- supply running water, reasonable hot water and heat, insofar as energy conditions permit;
- provide locks and keys if the tenant asks;
- and provide smoke detectors, and carbon monoxide detectors where the detector statute requires them.

You must deliver the unit in compliance with these duties at the start of the term; if you cannot, rent abates and the tenant may end the lease on 10 days' written notice and get back all prepaid rent and deposits.

No one may collect the rent free of these duties. Municipalities may add housing codes, and an unfit-dwelling ordinance can lead to repair or closing orders.
